+The Deformation card (Surface page) is ZBrush's Deformation sliders with Blender's mesh transforms and filters and Nomad's live filters: a list of what it does, the axes it works along where it takes any (X, Y and Z toggles; Offset, Rotate and Size take several, Skew, S bend and Squeeze one), and Amount, a slider to drag. Offset moves the free part along the axes by a share of its size (±100 %); Rotate turns it about its middle (±180°, about each axis in turn); Size scales it about its middle along the axes (−95 % to +200 %); Skew leans it along the axis the further the higher it is (along X over its width for Y; ±60°); S bend waves it once each way along the axis over its height (up to a quarter of its size); Squeeze pinches its middle across the axis, or bulges it the other way; Noise roughens the surface in and out along its normals with smooth bumps an eighth of its size across (up to 5 % of the size, the same bumps at the same place every time); Inflate pushes the surface out or in along its normals (up to 10 %); Spherize rounds the free part toward a ball as wide as it is (0 to 100 %). A drape under gravity is the Physics card's Settle, which lets the free part fall as cloth onto the floor and the other objects. These show live as the slider moves, set afresh each frame from where the drag began, as Nomad's filters do; letting go is one undo step and the slider springs back to 0 for the next, as the Deformers' Amount. Polish and Relax go over the surface's neighbours a number of times, so they run once, when the slider is let go, with the steps it shows (up to 20): Polish takes the roughness off as Smooth all does but keeps the size (Taubin's λ|μ smoothing, a step toward the neighbours' average then a slightly larger one back; on a rough sphere 10 steps keep the volume within 1 % where Smooth loses 6 %), Relax moves each vertex only along the surface toward its neighbours' average, which evens out their spacing and keeps the shape. Every one works on the part